From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Desidero <desidero@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 26 Nov 2026 07:29:54 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat(open-items): store a session's open items and serve them
 over the API

Agents label the questions, risks, findings and follow-ups they raise in
chat ([Q7] **Title** detail, ~~[Q7]~~ to close), and the web reads their
state back from the transcript. That state then depends on how much
history a browser has loaded, agents pick numbers from memory and reuse
them, and nothing outside the chat text can list or close an item.

This stores open items as rows of their session. The open_items side
store (docs/extending/side_schemas.md) keeps open_items_entries, keyed by
session, kind and number so the label is part of the key, and
open_items_heads with the next number per session and kind plus a
session row that counts the session's items. A raise locks the session
row and then the kind's row, so two raises at once never share a label;
each item carries an idempotency key, so a retried raise returns the
labels its first attempt allocated. Content (title, detail, topic, up to
eight links, a link item's URL, raised_by) and outcome (resolution,
closed_by, the latest five answers) are storage envelopes a deployment
may encrypt, so filtering beyond status and kind happens in the server
over the session's rows, which the 500-item cap bounds. Deleting a
session deletes its rows and heads through the side store's cleanup;
forks start empty.

Routes under /v1/sessions/{id}/open-items: GET lists with filters and
counts (read access); POST raises, only from the session's bound runner
and only for an agent whose spec keeps the new open_items tool group;
PATCH closes, reopens, retopics and relinks, from that runner or a
person with edit access; POST .../answers records a person's replies.
Every write publishes session.open_items.changed, and /v1/info reports
open_items. The agent tools and instruction follow in the next patches.

Not proposed upstream yet: upstream adds tables through its own
migration chain, so a proposal moves these two tables there.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Signed-off-by: Desidero <desidero@gmail.com>
